Date: Mon, 28 Aug 2017 09:34:21 +0000
Subject: Dirichlet distribution
Message-ID: <tEowA7rOkXY.mvBxY0BKCQAJ@tmb-users.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/tmb-users/c/tEowA7rOkXY/m/mvBxY0BKCQAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Dear TMB users,

I try to implement a dirichlet distribution as an observation equation in TMB.
As far as I see, this distribution is not implemented directly in TMB, but it is in ADMB.
I went through ADMB documentation to write this distribution in TMB.
My implementation of the log-likelihood works. I could retrieve the parameters of the dirichlet distribution.
However, I would like to write a cleaner code, maybe while created the dirichlet log-likelihood function prior to the model with which I can directly write my observations and parameters like:

nll += -ddirich(prop_observed, mu_prop, true)

Do you have any suggestions ?
Thank you.

Here is my code:
I calculate a dirichlet of `N` elements for each time step (t) between 1 and `nt`.
The original version is a model in JAGS written as:

for(t in 1:nt)
{
  prop_obs[t,1:N] ~ ddirich(mu.prop[t, 1:N])
}

Based on ADMB doc, the equation of dirichlet is:

 <https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/-LHyjdTjrQNI/WaPc_k7AbYI/AAAAAAAAAgM/4JRu_XNjRuYOvlevrf30KPDznoFKZkN1QCLcBGAs/s1600/ADMB.png>

I added some small values (EPS) in log calculations to avoid reaching the range of parameters during optimisation:

 <https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/-d5toKqwsF-s/WaPddiTnlDI/AAAAAAAAAgQ/Xpy-3AfXvMkkh4F3xwd_HVV7IkoZ8PyLQCLcBGAs/s1600/Negative_LL_TMB.png>

My proposition in TMB is the following:
- alpha in the above equation is named `mu_prop` below
- `p` in the above equation is named `prop_obs` below. This is the observation.

The equation has 3 parts:
- Gamma sum of each mu that will be `lgamma(mu_prop_bis.sum() + EPS)`
- Sum of each Gamma mu named `gammln_each_mu_prop.sum()`
- Sum of alpha * log observations that will be ` log_prop_obs_bis.sum()`

As I calculate also this for each time `t`, the script is a little bit more complicated.
Moreover, I sometimes transpose my matrices (then named `bis`) to be able to do sum calculations on columns as it did not work on rows.

for (r=0; r<N; ++r) {
  gammln_each_mu_prop(r,t)=lgamma(mu_prop(t,r) + EPS) ;
  mu_prop_bis(r,t)=mu_prop(t,r) ;
}

for (t = 0; t<nt; ++t) {
  for (r=0; r<N; ++r) {
    log_prop_obs_bis(r,t) = (mu_prop(t,r)-1.0)*log(prop_obs(t,r) + EPS);
  }
  // Dirichlet negative log-likelihood
  nll += -1.0*(lgamma(mu_prop_bis.col(t).sum() + EPS) - gammln_each_mu_F1_m_bis.col(t).sum() + log_prop_obs_bis.col(t).sum()) ;
}

